---
// Generic explainer for how sliding-scale and free-clinic pricing works.
// Used on the service+location page, the city hub, and the clinic detail page,
// so the copy stays consistent and only has to be updated in one place.
//
// `headingLevel` controls which heading tag this section's title is rendered as
// (2 → <h2>, 3 → <h3>, etc). Default is 2 — appropriate when the explainer is
// a top-level section of the page (service+location). When the explainer is
// nested inside another section (city hub's "Other ways to find care", clinic
// page's "Eligibility & cost"), pass 3 so the heading hierarchy stays correct.
export interface Props {
  headingLevel?: 2 | 3 | 4;
}
const { headingLevel = 2 } = Astro.props as Props;
const Heading = `h${headingLevel}` as 'h2' | 'h3' | 'h4';
---
<section class="sliding-scale" aria-labelledby="how-it-works">
  <Heading id="how-it-works" class="sliding-scale__title">How free and low-cost care works</Heading>
  <p>
    A "sliding-scale fee" means the clinic adjusts what you pay based on your
    household income and family size. If you make very little, many visits and
    basic services are free. If you earn more, you might pay $20–$60 for a
    visit instead of the $200–$400 a private clinic would charge. You will be
    asked to fill out a short form so the clinic can place you on the right
    tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    The phrase "free clinic" can be misleading. Most "free" clinics are
    actually sliding-scale clinics — completely free for the lowest income
    tier, and very low cost for everyone else. A handful of clinics, usually
    volunteer-run, are truly free for all patients regardless of income. Both
    kinds are listed here.
  </p>
  <p>
    To get the lowest fee, bring three things to your first visit: a
    <strong>photo ID</strong> (any government-issued ID),
    <strong>proof of income</strong> (a recent pay stub, an unemployment award
    letter, or a clinic-provided self-attestation form if you are paid in
    cash), and <strong>proof of address</strong> (a utility bill or any piece
    of mail). If you do not have these, most clinics will still see you — they
    just may charge you their standard sliding-scale rate until you can verify
    income.
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .sliding-scale {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }
  .sliding-scale__title {
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .sliding-scale p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
</style>
